{"abstention":null,"candidates":[{"claim":"A decentralized network is strictly more fragile than a large service with strict internal redundancy, because small services lack the economic slack to afford non-operational diversity and thus concentrate on the same minimum-cost dependencies.","counterconditions":["The large service's internal redundancy is merely logical replication on a single physical substrate","Operators possess private information leading to genuine substrate divergence","Regulation forces operator diversity","The large service is cost-constrained in the same way"],"mechanism":"Independent operators facing similar market pressures converge on identical commodity infrastructure (cloud availability zones, managed databases, core CDNs) because the cost of diverging exceeds the expected loss from a correlated outage. The large service has explicit economic slack to fund internal replication across diverse substrates, whereas small operators each optimize independently and converge to a monoculture.","optional_refs":["SRC_002","SRC_005","SRC_008"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["SRC_005","SRC_008"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"claim":"Both architectures are equally vulnerable to the worst-case correlated failure; decentralized networks merely obscure the correlation until a shared cause is found, while large services make the coupling explicit and thus auditable.","counterconditions":["Decentralized operators demonstrably use non-overlapping supply chains","The large service's internal coupling is not actually modeled or monitored","Independence is verified by active stress injection rather than assumed from topology"],"mechanism":"If a single root cause can fail one service, it can fail all services sharing the causal dependency (e.g., a global DNS provider, TLS certificate authority, or BGP route). In a large system, the internal coupling is explicit and can be modeled (e.g., 'all replicas depend on the same configuration service'), exposing the correlated failure mode to analysis. In a decentralized network, the causal chain is hidden across organizational boundaries, making the system appear independent until the shared dependency fails simultaneously, preventing proactive mitigation.","optional_refs":["SRC_007","SRC_008","SRC_002"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["SRC_002","SRC_007"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"claim":"Decentralization is more resilient only if a demonstrable causal independence barrier exists; without such a barrier, the large service's strictly greater internal redundancy dominates.","counterconditions":["The large service cannot achieve the same diversity due to internal standardization pressures","The causal barrier is not actually effective against the failure mode in question","The small services have less total redundancy than the large service"],"mechanism":"Redundancy mitigates independent failures, while causal independence barriers mitigate correlated failures. A large service can always replicate independent components (strictly more redundancy). Decentralization only wins if the topological separation creates a causal barrier the large service cannot achieve (e.g., different legal jurisdictions or air-gapped networks). Without a verified barrier, the large service's redundancy is strictly superior, as it is a superset of the small services' capacity.","optional_refs":["SRC_008","SRC_001","SRC_006"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["SRC_006","SRC_008"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.95},{"claim":"The distinction between 'independent' and 'merely uncorrelated' failure modes is only practically resolved by active stress injection across the full network, because passive observation cannot distinguish latent common causes from true independence without a triggered failure.","counterconditions":["Active stress injection is infeasible at scale","Latent causes are triggered by rare external events not reproducible in testing","The failure modes are truly independent and no shared stress triggers them"],"mechanism":"Latent common causes (shared configuration errors, latent bugs in common libraries) produce failures that appear uncorrelated until the trigger occurs. Passive observation of historical uptime cannot distinguish true independence from a latent common cause that has not yet been triggered (the 'black swan' problem). Active stress injection (e.g., mutating configurations, pushing resource limits) forces latent causes to manifest, allowing empirical discrimination. If independent services fail differently under identical stress, it evidences different causal paths.","optional_refs":["SRC_007","SRC_004","SRC_001"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["SRC_007","SRC_004"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"claim":"A decentralized network is more resilient to correlated failure if its operators are independently incentivized to maintain divergent operational practices, creating emergent diversity that a large service's internal alignment pressures eliminate.","counterconditions":["Operators are incentivized to conform to the same external standard (e.g., a dominant cloud provider's best practices)","The large service enforces internal diversity deliberately","Operational diversity is too small to prevent failure"],"mechanism":"A large service's internal coordination (e.g., standardization on tooling, centralized configuration management) is efficient but creates correlated failure modes because uniform configurations propagate errors uniformly. Decentralized operators lack this alignment and thus maintain diverse, often inefficient, operational practices. This diversity is an emergent causal barrier: because there is no single configuration management system, a configuration error cannot propagate across the network.","optional_refs":["SRC_003","SRC_005","SRC_006"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["SRC_003","SRC_006"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3},{"claim":"The evidence that distinguishes 'independent' from 'merely uncorrelated' is a rigorous dependency audit proving non-overlapping supply chains, because correlation in software systems is almost always caused by shared dependencies rather than coincidental independent bugs.","counterconditions":["A shared dependency is not the cause of a specific failure","Operators have proprietary, non-overlapping stacks but identical external interfaces","The correlation is caused by a shared external trigger rather than a shared internal component"],"mechanism":"Software failures are rarely truly independent due to the high complexity of modern systems. Apparent independence is often due to shared third-party libraries, OS kernels, or hardware architectures. A decentralized network's claim of independence is only substantiated by a dependency audit proving the operators do not share common-mode dependencies. If the audit reveals overlapping supply chains (e.g., all use the same OS version), the failures are not independent, regardless of organizational separation.","optional_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_007","SRC_004"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["SRC_005","SRC_004"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6}],"context_request":null,"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[{"member_refs":["NEW_001","NEW_002","SRC_007","SRC_008"],"seed_focus":"Distinguishing independence from latent correlation"}],"links":[{"direction":"directed","from_ref":"NEW_001","holds_when":"Evaluating the resilience of decentralized networks","relation_hint":"The counterexample in NEW_001 motivates the need for the test proposed in NEW_002.","to_ref":"NEW_002","weakens_when":"The test in NEW_002 is deemed infeasible"}],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"bears_on_refs":["SRC_008","SRC_006"],"content":"Counterexample candidate: A large service has N internal replicas on diverse hardware. A decentralized network has N small services, each using the same cloud provider's managed database. The cloud provider's managed database fails. The large service survives (it used diverse hardware). The decentralized network fails completely. The 'causal independence barrier' was an illusion of organizational separation, not a true topological barrier.","possible_next_move":"Formalize the conditions under which organizational separation does not imply causal independence.","why_keep_this":"Illustrates the core risk of assuming decentralization implies independence."},"local_key":"NEW_001"},{"body":{"bears_on_refs":["SRC_007","SRC_004"],"content":"Hypothesis: Active stress injection is the only practical way to distinguish 'independent' from 'uncorrelated until triggered'. If you inject the same stress (e.g., latency, packet loss, resource exhaustion) into all services, and they fail in different ways, it evidences different causal paths. If they fail in the same way, it suggests a shared dependency or design.","possible_next_move":"Design a simulation to test the discriminative power of active stress injection.","why_keep_this":"Provides a concrete empirical test for the abstract distinction asked in the prompt."},"local_key":"NEW_002"}],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Can a large service always achieve strictly more internal redundancy, or are there topological limits to internal diversity (e.g., a single configuration management system) that a decentralized network naturally avoids?","related_refs":["SRC_006","SRC_008"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}